# Family Affairs — optional network configuration.
#
# The game needs NONE of this to run. With an empty config it uses PeerJS's free
# public broker and Google's public STUN servers, which is what the live site
# does today. Everything below is here so that scaling up is a rebuild, not a
# rewrite.
#
# Copy to `.env.local` for local builds. For the deployed site, set the same
# names as GitHub Actions repository secrets — the deploy workflow passes them
# through if they exist and ignores them if they do not.

# --- TURN relay ------------------------------------------------------------
# The single highest-value thing to add. STUN alone connects most players
# directly, but roughly 10-20% — symmetric NAT, strict corporate wifi, some
# mobile carriers — cannot be connected without a relay, and for them the game
# does not work at all. Free tiers exist (Cloudflare Calls, Metered) and a card
# game's traffic is tiny; it is a handful of small JSON messages per turn, not
# video.
#
# These credentials ship to the browser. That is how WebRTC works and is why
# providers issue short-lived ones. Treat them as a rate limit, not a password.
#
# Metered: dashboard -> TURN Server -> Add Credential -> Show ICE Servers Array.
# The URLs are identical for every account; only the username and credential are
# yours. Four entries because different networks block different ports, and
# having the TLS-over-443 one is most of the point.
#
#   VITE_TURN_URLS=turn:global.relay.metered.ca:80,turn:global.relay.metered.ca:80?transport=tcp,turn:global.relay.metered.ca:443,turns:global.relay.metered.ca:443?transport=tcp
#
VITE_TURN_URLS=
VITE_TURN_USERNAME=
VITE_TURN_CREDENTIAL=

# --- STUN ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Comma-separated. Defaults to Google's public servers when empty.
VITE_STUN_URLS=

# --- Signalling broker -----------------------------------------------------
# Only brokers the introduction between two browsers; no game traffic passes
# through it. If it goes down, running games are unaffected and new ones cannot
# start. Leave empty to use the public PeerJS broker.
#
# To self-host: `npx peer --port 9000 --key peerjs --path /` behind TLS, then
# point these at it. It is a small stateless Node process and fits comfortably
# in a free or near-free tier.
VITE_PEER_HOST=
VITE_PEER_PORT=443
VITE_PEER_PATH=/
VITE_PEER_SECURE=true

# --- Hosting ---------------------------------------------------------------
# GitHub Pages serves from /<repo>/, so the deploy workflow sets this itself.
# A root-served host (Cloudflare Pages, Netlify, Vercel) wants "/".
VITE_BASE=/
